WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.954
Data is facts and numbers we collect.

2
00:00:07.174 --> 00:00:11.772
Today you are a data detective. You
read it, and a computer sorts it.

3
00:00:12.272 --> 00:00:15.755
Your name is data. Your
favourite colour is data too.

4
00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:20.061
How many pets you have is
data. So is how tall you are.

5
00:00:20.281 --> 00:00:24.693
Some children think data must be
numbers. It can be words as well.

6
00:00:24.913 --> 00:00:29.371
Once we have collected data, a
computer can help us look at it.

7
00:00:29.871 --> 00:00:35.212
A computer can answer many kinds of
question. Each kind a different way.

8
00:00:35.432 --> 00:00:38.822
Will it rain tomorrow? That is
a question for the weather app.

9
00:00:39.042 --> 00:00:42.432
Which way is the park?
The map app knows the way.

10
00:00:42.652 --> 00:00:45.857
How do you spell elephant?
Search the web for that.

11
00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:50.953
The web does not know what our class
likes. Only our own table knows that.

12
00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:55.261
A form is a page where we
put data into a computer.

13
00:00:55.481 --> 00:01:00.496
Amal says bananas. You tap Banana
on the form, then press Submit.

14
00:01:00.716 --> 00:01:05.546
Record what the person said. Your
own favourite is not their data.

15
00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:10.085
Six friends, six answers. The
computer keeps every single one.

16
00:01:10.585 --> 00:01:13.882
The computer puts every
answer into a table for us.

17
00:01:14.102 --> 00:01:18.328
Apple two. Banana three.
Mango one. Orange none.

18
00:01:18.548 --> 00:01:22.542
Which fruit did the most children
choose? Banana, with three.

19
00:01:22.762 --> 00:01:27.871
Did anyone choose orange? No. The
orange row says nobody chose it.

20
00:01:28.091 --> 00:01:33.199
A table only knows what is in it. Amal's
favourite song is not in the table.

21
00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:37.275
Sorting means putting things
into groups that belong together.

22
00:01:37.495 --> 00:01:41.210
Here are twelve things, all
mixed up. Sort them by colour.

23
00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:46.399
Reds together, yellows together,
greens, oranges. Done in a blink.

24
00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:50.984
Now sort the same things by type.
Fruit here, vegetables there.

25
00:01:51.204 --> 00:01:55.663
There is no one right way. Computers
help us sort and organise data.

26
00:01:56.163 --> 00:01:59.321
A table has rows and
columns. Rows go across.

27
00:01:59.541 --> 00:02:03.720
Columns go down. One column
holds one fact about everybody.

28
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.191
Want to know what Amal
likes? Read along Amal's row.

29
00:02:07.411 --> 00:02:11.451
Knowing which way to read is most
of the job of a data detective.

30
00:02:11.951 --> 00:02:14.691
Data is the facts and numbers we collect.

31
00:02:14.911 --> 00:02:18.812
Ask a computer the right way,
and record the answers on a form.

32
00:02:19.032 --> 00:02:23.537
Read the table across a row, or
down a column, and it answers you.

33
00:02:23.757 --> 00:02:29.655
A computer sorts and organises data
in a blink. You are a data detective.
